# CustodyChain — Track H: Media & Civic Trust

A forensic evidence-integrity system: cryptographic hashing, a tamper-evident
(hash-chained) chain of custody, and a plain-English verification report for
non-technical stakeholders — built to the brief in `cybersecurity_hackathon.pdf`.

This README maps every design decision back to that document so a judge can
follow the thread from "Rule 05 / Rule 06" straight to the code.

## What's actually proven to work, not just built

Every claim below was tested against the running system, not just written
into the code and assumed correct:

- **File tampering is caught.** Uploaded a real file, verified it clean
  (AUTHENTIC), changed one word, re-verified — the system correctly flagged
  TAMPERED and showed both hashes.
- **Database tampering is caught.** Edited a custody-log row directly with
  raw SQL (bypassing the API entirely — this is Day 6's red-team task), then
  asked the API to re-check the chain. It found the exact entry that had
  been altered and the judicial report correctly flipped to
  "INTEGRITY COMPROMISED."
- **The frontend actually talks to the backend.** A headless integration
  test (`frontend/test_frontend.mjs`) drives the real UI — upload, verify
  clean, verify tampered, pull the judicial report — against the live API
  and checks the rendered output, not just that requests were sent.

## Architecture

```
backend/           FastAPI + SQLite — the cryptographic core
  app/hashing.py      chunked SHA-256/BLAKE2b, hash-chain logic, Merkle tree
  app/database.py     SQLite schema (evidence + custody_log tables)
  app/metadata.py     read-only ExifTool metadata extraction
  app/main.py         API endpoints
  seed_demo_data.py   Faker-generated synthetic evidence (no real data)

frontend/           Static HTML/CSS/JS — no build step
  index.html          two views: Field Officer console + Judicial report
  app.js              upload / verify / offline-queue / report rendering
  test_frontend.mjs   headless integration test (see above)

demo/custodychain_demo.html
  A single self-contained file with no backend at all — the same two
  views, but hashing happens with the browser's own Web Crypto API and
  the custody log is kept in the file's own storage. Open it directly
  in any browser to try the whole flow (upload, verify, tamper, red-team)
  with zero setup. Useful for a pitch laptop with no Wi-Fi in the room.

public/            Public landing page + self serve verify flow for
                     journalists, fact checkers, and citizens — added
                     later, strictly as new code on top of the backend
                     above. See public/README.md for the full account of
                     what was and wasn't touched to build it, including
                     two real bugs found and fixed during testing (a
                     text-diff granularity bug and an image-message
                     miscalibration) and one spec edge case that doesn't
                     map cleanly onto this design.
```

No npm build, no bundler, for the frontend — see "Honest limits" for why.

## How the brief maps to this build

| Brief item | Where it lives |
|---|---|
| Data Integrity, Non-Repudiation, Chain of Custody | `hashing.py` — SHA-256 + BLAKE2b digest, hash-chained log |
| Rule 05 (Honest Limits) | See below |
| Rule 06 (Offline First) | `app.js` offline queue + `POST /sync/offline-queue` |
| Chunked hashing for huge files | `hashing.hash_and_save_upload` reads in 8 MiB chunks, never buffers a whole file |
| Hash-chained audit log | `_append_custody_entry` — each entry embeds the previous entry's hash |
| ExifTool metadata | `app/metadata.py`, read-only, never writes to the evidence file |
| Merkle tree | `hashing.merkle_root` / `GET /merkle/root` |
| Faker synthetic data | `seed_demo_data.py` |
| Officer Field Interface | `index.html` "Field officer" view |
| Judicial / Panel View | `index.html` "Judicial review" view — plain-English status, timeline |
| Red-team day (Day 6) | See "Live demo script" below |

## Running it

**Backend:**
```bash
cd backend
pip install -r requirements.txt --break-system-packages   # or use a venv
python seed_demo_data.py      # optional: loads synthetic demo cases
uvicorn app.main:app --reload --port 8000
```

**Frontend** (needs a static server, not `file://`, so `fetch()` isn't
blocked by the browser's file-origin restrictions):
```bash
cd frontend
python3 -m http.server 5500
# open http://127.0.0.1:5500
```

If your API isn't on `127.0.0.1:8000`, set it before the page loads:
```html
<script>window.CUSTODYCHAIN_API = "http://your-host:8000";</script>
```

**Optional — re-run the integration test:**
```bash
cd frontend
npm install        # installs jsdom, dev-only
npm test           # drives the real UI against your running backend
```

## Live demo script (Day 7 pitch, as written in the brief)

1. Open the **Field officer** view. Fill in a case ID and upload any file —
   watch the hash appear in the ledger instantly.
2. Turn off your Wi-Fi. Upload another file — the app hashes it locally
   with `crypto.subtle.digest` and queues it (Rule 06). Turn Wi-Fi back on;
   it syncs automatically.
3. Copy the evidence ID from the ledger. In a terminal, flip one byte of
   the same file you uploaded, then use the **"Compare against original"**
   form with the modified file. Watch the verdict flip to **TAMPERED**.
4. Switch to the **Judicial review** view, paste the evidence ID. The seal
   badge renders visibly cracked and the report explains *why* in plain
   language — no hash talk required.
5. For the red-team flourish: open `backend/custodychain.db` with any
   SQLite tool and edit a `custody_log` row directly (bypassing the app).
   Reload the judicial report — it now reads "INTEGRITY COMPROMISED" and
   names the exact tampered entry.

## Honest limits (Rule 05)

Being upfront about what this system does and doesn't guarantee:

- **SHA-256 collision resistance vs. disk I/O speed.** SHA-256 is
  computationally collision-resistant for the foreseeable future, but on
  very large files (100+ GB) the bottleneck in practice is disk read
  throughput, not the hash function — chunked reading bounds RAM use, not
  wall-clock time.
- **The hash chain proves the log wasn't edited after the fact — it
  doesn't prove the first entry was honest.** If an officer hashes a file
  *after* already tampering with it in the field, the chain will faithfully
  record that (wrong) hash as ground truth. This system detects tampering
  *after* collection; it can't detect tampering that happens *before* the
  first hash is taken. Provenance at the point of capture (e.g. a bodycam
  with hardware-level signing) is a separate, harder problem this project
  doesn't attempt to solve.
- **CORS is wide open (`allow_origins=["*"]`)** for demo convenience. A
  real deployment would restrict this to the actual officer/judicial
  frontend origins.
- **No authentication/authorization layer.** Anyone who can reach the API
  can upload evidence or add custody events. A production system needs
  officer/judge identity verified independently of whatever name they type
  into a form field — right now `actor` and `officer_name` are trusted
  input, not verified identity.
- **Offline sync trusts the client-computed hash.** When a device syncs
  hashes computed while offline, the server has no way to confirm those
  bytes weren't altered between hashing and syncing, beyond the device's
  own integrity. This mirrors real evidence-handling: trust ultimately
  traces back to the collecting officer at some point, and the system is
  explicit about where that trust boundary sits rather than pretending it
  doesn't exist.
- **No build step for the frontend, on purpose.** The brief suggests
  React; this ships as plain HTML/CSS/JS instead so a judge can open it
  and read exactly what's running without a bundler in the way. The same
  logic would port to a React component tree with no change to the API
  contract.
